.TH TUI_DIALOG_MESSAGE 3 2026-10-03 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_dialog_message, tui_dialog_confirm, tui_dialog_prompt, tui_dialog_menu,
tui_dialog_checklist, tui_dialog_is_checked, tui_dialog_help, tui_dialog_free,
tui_dialog_handle, tui_dialog_render \- modal dialogs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/dialog.h>
.PP
.BI "int  tui_dialog_message(tui_dialog *" d ", const char *" title ", const char *" text );
.BI "int  tui_dialog_confirm(tui_dialog *" d ", const char *" title ", const char *" text ,
.BI "                        int " default_yes );
.BI "int  tui_dialog_prompt(tui_dialog *" d ", const char *" title ", const char *" text ,
.BI "                       char *" buf ", size_t " size ", const char *" initial );
.BI "int  tui_dialog_menu(tui_dialog *" d ", const char *" title ,
.BI "                     const char *const *" items ", int " n ", int " selected );
.BI "int  tui_dialog_checklist(tui_dialog *" d ", const char *" title ,
.BI "                          const char *const *" items ", int " n ,
.BI "                          const unsigned char *" initial ", int " selected );
.BI "int  tui_dialog_is_checked(const tui_dialog *" d ", int " i );
.BI "int  tui_dialog_help(tui_dialog *" d ", const char *" title ,
.BI "                     const char *const *" pairs ", int " n );
.BI "void tui_dialog_free(tui_dialog *" d );
.BI "tui_dialog_state tui_dialog_handle(tui_dialog *" d ", const tui_event *" ev );
.BI "void tui_dialog_render(tui_dialog *" d ", tui_canvas " c );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A dialog neither blocks nor reads the terminal.
While one is open, the program gives it the events first
.RB ( tui_dialog_handle ())
and draws it last
.RB ( tui_dialog_render ()),
and reads the outcome once
.I d\->state
is no longer
.BR TUI_DIALOG_OPEN :
.B TUI_DIALOG_OK
or
.BR TUI_DIALOG_CANCELLED .
The decision stays in the program's own model, as in issue-manager.
.TP
.BR tui_dialog_message ()
Text and an OK button; Enter, Space or Esc closes it with
.BR TUI_DIALOG_OK .
.TP
.BR tui_dialog_confirm ()
Text and Yes / No.
y and n answer at once; Left, Right and Tab move between the buttons and
Enter takes the focused one; Esc and Ctrl-C cancel.
.I d\->answer
is 1 for yes, 0 for no (and 0 when cancelled).
.TP
.BR tui_dialog_prompt ()
Text and one line of input, edited in the caller's
.I buf
by a
.BR tui_lineedit_init (3)
with all its keys; Enter confirms, Esc and Ctrl-C cancel.
.TP
.BR tui_dialog_menu ()
A list of
.I n
items (not copied; the caller keeps them alive) with the list keys of
.BR tui_listview_handle (3);
Enter chooses
.RI ( d\->answer
is the index), Esc cancels.
.TP
.BR tui_dialog_checklist ()
A list of
.I n
items in which several can be ticked: each row shows "[x]" or "[ ]",
Space ticks or unticks the row the list stands on, the list keys move,
Enter confirms and Esc cancels.
.I initial
(may be NULL: nothing ticked) gives each item's first state and is copied,
so a cancelled checklist leaves the caller's set as it was.
On
.B TUI_DIALOG_OK
the ticked set is
.I d\->checked
\(em one byte per item, 1 when ticked \(em or
.BR tui_dialog_is_checked ();
.I d\->answer
is the row Enter was pressed on, the single choice a program can fall back
to when nothing is ticked (issue-manager's status filter does).
.TP
.BR tui_dialog_help ()
The help screen of a view, as the workspace's interface policy describes
it: a box over the view, titled with the view's name
(e.g. "Message list shortcuts"), with
.I n
rows of a key in bold and what it does beside it.
.I pairs
holds 2 *
.I n
strings, key and description in turn; they are not copied, and a NULL
one is drawn empty.
Esc,
.B q
and Backspace close it with
.BR TUI_DIALOG_OK ,
as they go back from any view, and so does Ctrl\-C; the list's movement
keys (Up, Down, PgUp, PgDn, Home, End,
.BR j ", " k ", " ctrl\-f ", " ctrl\-b ", " g ", " G )
scroll it when the rows do not all fit; every other key changes nothing,
except
.BR / ,
which opens a one-line search.
While the user types, the rows narrow to the entries (a key and what it does,
shown whole or not at all) whose key or text contains every typed word,
in either case; the matched words are drawn underlined, bold and in the
accent colour, and the line (\(lq/ words\(rq) is the last row of the box.
Enter leaves the line and keeps the narrowed list; Esc clears the search first
and closes the help on the next press; a search with no match shows
\(lqNo key matches "words"\(rq.
The line is kept in the dialog's
.I checked
array, which a help has no other use for; a failed allocation leaves
.B /
without effect.
.PP
.BR tui_dialog_render ()
draws a box centred in
.IR c ,
sized to the text, the title and the buttons or items, and to at most the
canvas less a margin of one cell; the text is wrapped to the box.
Short of rows, the blank line above the buttons goes first, so the text
keeps at least one row.
A prompt puts the terminal cursor in its input line; the other kinds hide
it.
The styles of the frame, the inside and the focused button or selected
item are
.IR d\->frame ,
.I d\->fill
and
.I d\->focus
(by default the frame in
.BR TUI_STYLE_ACCENT ,
the inside plain, the focus in
.BR TUI_STYLE_SELECTION ;
see
.BR tui_screen_new (3))
and may be changed after the constructor.
The buttons are drawn in the focus style as it is.
A menu or checklist row is drawn, when the focus is
.BR TUI_STYLE_SELECTION ,
in the selection of
.BR tui_table_init (3):
the whole row on a dark blue background (256 or 16 colours, from
.B COLORTERM
and
.BR TERM ;
bold only on an 8 colour terminal), the text keeping its colour, and a
bold \(aq>\(aq in the first column with the text from the third.
Any other focus style is the program\(aqs own and is used as it is.
.PP
.BR tui_dialog_free ()
releases what the dialog allocated; call it when the dialog is done.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The constructors return 0, or \-1 with
.I errno
.B EINVAL
(no buffer, items missing) or
.BR ENOMEM .
.BR tui_dialog_handle ()
returns the state after the event; events after the dialog closed are
ignored.
.BR tui_dialog_is_checked ()
returns 0 for an index out of range and for a dialog that is not a
checklist.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
if (TUI_EV_IS_CHAR(&ev, 'q')) {
    tui_dialog_confirm(&dlg, "Quit", "Leave the program?", 0);
    dialog_open = 1;
} else if (dialog_open &&
           tui_dialog_handle(&dlg, &ev) != TUI_DIALOG_OPEN) {
    quit = dlg.state == TUI_DIALOG_OK && dlg.answer;
    tui_dialog_free(&dlg);
    dialog_open = 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_lineedit_init (3),
.BR tui_listview_init (3),
.BR libtuikit (7)
